WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.487
Text validation is more complex than checking whether a string is nonempty.

2
00:00:04.487 --> 00:00:13.086
The program has to know what encoding it expects, how invalid byte sequences are handled, which characters are allowed, what length means, and

3
00:00:13.086 --> 00:00:16.077
whether comparisons are case-sensitive or locale-sensitive.

4
00:00:16.077 --> 00:00:19.068
Different components can interpret the same text differently.

5
00:00:19.068 --> 00:00:24.302
One layer may preserve whitespace, another may trim it, and another may fold case.

6
00:00:24.302 --> 00:00:29.537
One component may treat two representations as equivalent while another treats them as different.

7
00:00:29.537 --> 00:00:33.649
Validation should happen on the form the program will actually use.

8
00:00:33.649 --> 00:00:38.510
Canonicalization means converting input into a consistent representation before comparison or policy decisions.

9
00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:45.988
That can include trimming where appropriate, normalizing case for identifiers that are case-insensitive, applying a documented Unicode or locale

10
00:00:45.988 --> 00:00:50.474
policy, or resolving a path-like value through a defensive, approved API.

11
00:00:50.474 --> 00:00:52.344
Whitespace rules should be explicit.

12
00:00:52.344 --> 00:01:00.569
Leading spaces, trailing spaces, tabs, line endings, empty strings, and visually similar values can create confusion if each component makes its own

13
00:01:00.569 --> 00:01:00.943
decision.

14
00:01:00.943 --> 00:01:05.056
Decide whether whitespace is meaningful, normalized, or rejected for each field.

15
00:01:05.056 --> 00:01:07.673
Path-like values should remain boundary-controlled.

16
00:01:07.673 --> 00:01:13.281
Treat them as structured inputs with expected base locations, allowed forms, and canonical comparison rules.

17
00:01:13.281 --> 00:01:21.133
Keep this defensive: the goal is to ensure the program works with the intended canonical value, not a differently interpreted string.

18
00:01:21.133 --> 00:01:26.741
After text is validated and canonicalized, store the accepted representation separately from the raw string.

19
00:01:26.741 --> 00:01:36.462
Reviewers should be able to identify which string is raw input, which one is canonical, and which one is safe for the program's specific use.

20
00:01:36.462 --> 00:01:42.070
Be especially careful when values pass between libraries, operating system APIs, databases, and user interfaces.

21
00:01:42.070 --> 00:01:48.800
Each layer may have its own comparison and normalization rules, so centralizing the accepted text policy reduces disagreement.
